GUIDES = [
dict(slug='guide-virtual-event-production.html', pillar='pre-production',
 title='How to Plan a Virtual Event: The Complete Production Guide (2026) | Virtual Studio Events',
 desc='Step-by-step virtual event production planning from broadcast engineers: audience, format, run order, rehearsals, redundancy, crew and measurement.',
 h1='How to plan a virtual event: <span class="em">the production guide.</span>', h1_plain='How to plan a virtual event: the production guide',
 lede='The practical checklist we run on every show — written for the person whose name is on the invite.',
 related=['guide-run-order.html','guide-rehearsals.html','guide-risk-redundancy.html','guide-live-streaming-cost.html'],
 body='''
<p>Most virtual events fail in planning, not on the day. The stream that freezes, the speaker who can't be heard, the Q&amp;A nobody moderated — each traces back to a decision that wasn't made six weeks earlier. This guide is the sequence we follow on every production, from the first call to the post-event report.</p>
<h2>Start with the audience, not the technology</h2>
<p>Before anyone mentions a platform, answer three questions. Who is watching? Where are they watching — at a desk, on a phone on a train, on a screen in a warehouse canteen? And what must they be able to <em>do</em>: just watch, or ask questions, vote, network, download? Every subsequent decision — format, platform, crew, budget — flows from those answers. A town hall for 4,000 shift workers on mobile is a different production from a 40-person investor briefing, even if both are "a virtual event".</p>
<h2>Choose the format deliberately</h2>
<p>Single-session events suit a <strong>broadcast</strong> approach: one polished stream, a strong presenter, a tight run order and minimal audience friction. Multi-track conferences need a <strong>platform</strong> with an agenda, session rooms and registration. Awards shows are a third thing entirely — they live or die on pace, so the risky elements are pre-recorded and the live moments are kept live. Decide the format first and let it dictate the tools, never the reverse. Our <a href="guide-virtual-event-platform.html">platform guide</a> covers when you genuinely don't need one.</p>
<h2>Build a real run order</h2>
<p>A run order is a minute-by-minute account of the show: who is on, what is on screen, which source, which microphone, what plays next, and who calls it. It is the single document that lets a gallery <em>anticipate</em> rather than react. If your production company doesn't ask you for one — or offer to write it — be nervous. We've published the <a href="guide-run-order.html">full run order method</a> and a <a href="templates.html">downloadable template</a>.</p>
<h2>Rehearse the failure, not just the show</h2>
<p>A technical rehearsal tests every remote speaker on the actual kit they'll use on the day: same laptop, same room, same connection, same headphones. It also tests what happens when something breaks — the backup presenter link, the holding slide, the second internet path. We run every show with redundant encoders and a second connection because sooner or later one of them is needed. <a href="guide-rehearsals.html">What to test, and when</a>.</p>
<h2>Pre-record strategically</h2>
<p>Anything that cannot be allowed to fail — the CEO's keynote, the awards montage, the product reveal — is a candidate for pre-recording with a live Q&amp;A afterwards. Audiences readily accept a polished pre-record; they don't accept a frozen keynote. The trick is to pre-record in the same set, with the same lighting and framing as the live segments, so the join is invisible.</p>
<h2>Crew it properly</h2>
<p>The minimum viable crew for a professional virtual event is three people: a vision mixer or vMix operator running the pictures, a producer calling the show and watching the clock, and a speaker wrangler managing the humans. One person cannot do all three well, and on show day "well" is the only acceptable standard. Bigger shows add a graphics operator, a sound engineer and a platform moderator. Our <a href="guide-gallery-roles.html">gallery roles guide</a> explains each job.</p>
<h2>Plan the interaction, not just the broadcast</h2>
<p>Q&amp;A, polls and chat each need an owner. Who moderates questions? Who decides which reach the stage? Who launches the poll on cue, and who reads out the result? Interaction that isn't staffed becomes noise, and noise reads as chaos. <a href="guide-live-interaction.html">How to run it live</a>.</p>
<h2>Decide what success looks like before you go live</h2>
<p>Peak concurrent viewers, average watch time, Q&amp;A volume, drop-off points, on-demand views in the week after. Set up the analytics before the event and agree the metrics with stakeholders in advance — otherwise the post-event conversation becomes an argument about which numbers count. <a href="guide-event-metrics.html">Metrics that matter</a> and <a href="guide-post-event-report.html">the report to write</a>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>The one-line version:</strong> know your audience, pick the format, write the run order, rehearse the failure, pre-record the un-droppable, crew properly, staff the interaction, agree the numbers.</p></div>
'''),

dict(slug='guide-run-order.html', pillar='pre-production',
 title='How to Write a Run Order for a Live or Virtual Event (With Template) | Virtual Studio Events',
 desc='The run order is the most important document in live production. What goes in it, how broadcast crews format it, common mistakes, and a free template to download.',
 h1='How to write a run order <span class="em">the gallery can actually use.</span>', h1_plain='How to write a run order the gallery can actually use',
 lede='The single document that turns a nervous event into a calm one — and why most organisers write it wrong.',
 related=['guide-show-calling-comms.html','guide-rehearsals.html','guide-virtual-event-production.html'],
 body='''
<p>An agenda tells the audience what happens. A run order tells the crew <em>how</em>. The difference matters: "10:00 Welcome from the CEO" is an agenda line; the run order version is four lines covering the walk-on music, the camera shot, the name strap, the microphone and who cues her.</p>
<h2>What a run order contains</h2>
<p>Every row is one thing that happens, in time order. The columns crews expect are: <strong>item number</strong> (so people can say "we're on item 14" on comms); <strong>start time and duration</strong>; <strong>the item itself</strong> in plain words; <strong>source</strong> — which camera, playback, remote feed or screen share; <strong>audio</strong> — which mics are live, whether a video has embedded sound; <strong>graphics</strong> — lower thirds, holding slides, poll overlays; <strong>who is on</strong>; and <strong>notes</strong> for anything unusual. Long shows also carry a running "actual" time column that the producer fills in live to track drift.</p>
<h2>Granularity: minutes, not sessions</h2>
<p>The most common mistake is writing the run order at session level. A 45-minute panel isn't one line — it's the intro (presenter, cam 1, name strap), the panel wide (cam 2, all mics live), the pre-recorded VT in the middle (playback, embedded audio, mics muted), the audience Q&amp;A (moderator on cam 1, questions on screen from the platform), the wrap (presenter, cam 1) and the transition to the break (holding slide, music bed). Each of those is a cue, and every cue is a row.</p>
<h2>Timings that survive contact with reality</h2>
<p>Build in buffers at the moments that always overrun: after keynotes, before breaks, at the end of Q&amp;A. Mark which items are <strong>fixed</strong> (the 12:00 announcement that's been trailed to the press) and which are <strong>flexible</strong> (the panel can lose five minutes). When the show runs late — it will — the producer already knows where to claw time back.</p>
<h2>The pre-show and post-show belong in it</h2>
<p>The run order starts at crew call, not at the top of the show. Line check, remote speaker checks, platform go-live, holding loop, the "two minutes to live" call. It ends after the last viewer sees the last slide: the closing graphic, the stream stop, the recording save, the platform close. If it isn't written down, someone forgets to stop the recording — we've seen the 90-minute empty-studio recordings.</p>
<h2>Versioning and distribution</h2>
<p>Number your versions and put the version and date in the header. Circulate a locked version 24 hours before show day; changes after that are called on comms and pencilled in, never re-issued as a new document during the show. Print copies for the gallery. Screens fail; paper doesn't.</p>
<h2>Common mistakes we see</h2>
<ul>
<li>No item numbers, so nobody can reference a cue quickly on comms.</li>
<li>Sources missing — "CEO keynote" without saying whether she's in the room, remote, or pre-recorded.</li>
<li>Audio ignored entirely; the graphics operator plays a VT and the room hears nothing.</li>
<li>No buffers, so a five-minute overrun at 10 a.m. wrecks the whole afternoon.</li>
<li>Different departments working from different versions.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><p>Download our <a href="templates.html">run order template</a> — the same layout we use in the gallery — and adapt it to your show.</p></div>
'''),

dict(slug='guide-speaker-prep.html', pillar='pre-production',
 title='Preparing Remote Speakers for a Virtual Event: Kit, Connection & Coaching | Virtual Studio Events',
 desc='How to get remote presenters looking and sounding professional: the equipment brief, connection checks, framing and lighting, rehearsal and what to send them the night before.',
 h1='Remote speakers: <span class="em">the ten-minute brief that saves the show.</span>', h1_plain='Remote speakers: the ten-minute brief that saves the show',
 lede='Your remote presenter is a one-person outside broadcast unit who has never done this before. Here\'s how to set them up to succeed.',
 related=['guide-remote-contribution.html','guide-rehearsals.html','guide-presenter-on-camera.html'],
 body='''
<p>The weakest link in most virtual events isn't the platform or the encoder — it's the keynote speaker joining from a hotel room on Wi-Fi with a laptop microphone. Everything that follows is what we send to and check with every remote contributor, in the order that matters.</p>
<h2>1. Connection first, everything else second</h2>
<p>A wired Ethernet connection beats any Wi-Fi. If wired isn't possible, the speaker sits close to the router, and everyone else in the building stops streaming for the duration. We ask for a speed test from the exact spot they'll present from — we want at least 10 Mbps upload for a good HD contribution, and we want to know what happens when a second device joins. Phone tethering makes a good backup path, and we test it.</p>
<h2>2. Audio is 80% of perceived quality</h2>
<p>A wired headset or a USB microphone transforms a contribution; a laptop's built-in mic in an echoey room ruins it. Headphones are non-negotiable: without them the speaker's speakers feed back into their mic and the whole broadcast gets an echo. We send a short list of affordable USB mics and, for keynote-level speakers, courier a kit.</p>
<h2>3. Camera, framing and light</h2>
<p>Camera at eye level — a stack of books under the laptop is fine. Light in front of the face, not behind it; a window in front of them is the best free key light there is. Frame with a little headroom, eyes in the top third. The background should be tidy and, ideally, real — virtual backgrounds flicker at the edges and look like exactly what they are.</p>
<h2>4. The environment</h2>
<p>Door closed, notifications off, phone on silent, a note on the door. If they're presenting from an office, ask IT to whitelist the contribution platform in advance — corporate firewalls are the most common cause of "it worked at home".</p>
<h2>5. Rehearse on the real kit</h2>
<p>Every remote speaker gets a technical check on the same laptop, in the same room, with the same connection they'll use live. A "quick test from my phone" tells us nothing. The check covers audio levels, framing, screen-share if they're presenting slides, and the return feed so they can see and hear the show.</p>
<h2>6. Slides: take them off the speaker's machine</h2>
<p>Wherever possible we run presenters' slides from the gallery, on cue from the speaker. It removes screen-share bandwidth, prevents notification pop-ups on screen, and lets us hold the last slide if their connection drops. The speaker sees a return feed of their slides and simply says "next".</p>
<h2>7. The night-before email</h2>
<p>Join link, join time (30 minutes before their slot), the phone number of a human in the gallery, a one-line reminder of the kit checklist, and what to do if the link fails — usually a dial-in phone number as a last resort so their voice at least can be heard over a holding slide.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> if a speaker hasn't been tested on their real setup, assume they will fail, and have a plan for it in the run order.</p></div>
'''),

dict(slug='guide-rehearsals.html', pillar='pre-production',
 title='Technical Rehearsals for Live & Virtual Events: What to Test and When | Virtual Studio Events',
 desc='How professional crews rehearse virtual and hybrid events: line checks, tech runs, dress rehearsals, remote speaker checks, failure drills and the rehearsal schedule that works.',
 h1='Rehearsals: <span class="em">test the show, then break it on purpose.</span>', h1_plain='Rehearsals: test the show, then break it on purpose',
 lede='What a proper technical rehearsal covers, how long it takes, and the failure drills most events skip.',
 related=['guide-risk-redundancy.html','guide-speaker-prep.html','guide-run-order.html','guide-when-it-goes-wrong.html'],
 body='''
<p>"We'll do a quick run-through at nine" is how most virtual events rehearse, and it's why so many go wrong at ten. A proper rehearsal is three distinct activities with different goals, and each catches different problems.</p>
<h2>The tech run: does everything connect?</h2>
<p>This happens the day before, or first thing on show day for simpler events. Every source is brought up in turn: each camera, each playback item, each remote contributor, each screen-share, each graphic. Audio is checked on every route — room, stream, remote returns. The stream is started to a private test destination and watched on a phone over mobile data, not on the gallery monitor. Nobody performs; the goal is purely to prove every path works.</p>
<h2>The dress rehearsal: does the show flow?</h2>
<p>Now the run order is followed top to bottom in real time, with presenters. Transitions are the point — the handover from a VT back to a live presenter, the moment a remote speaker is brought in, the poll launch. Timings are checked against the run order and buffers adjusted. For a 90-minute show, budget two hours; overruns in rehearsal are information, not failure.</p>
<h2>The failure drill: what happens when it breaks?</h2>
<p>This is the one events skip, and it's the one that separates crews. During the dress run, the producer deliberately kills things: the primary encoder is stopped, a remote speaker's link is dropped, a playback file is "missing". The team practises the response — switch to the backup encoder, bring up the holding slide, go to the alternative item — until it's boring. On the day, a real failure then becomes a rehearsed cue rather than a crisis.</p>
<h2>Remote speakers: individual checks, then a group check</h2>
<p>Each remote contributor gets a one-to-one technical check in the days before (see <a href="guide-speaker-prep.html">speaker prep</a>). On show day they join 30 minutes before their slot for a line check — audio, picture, return feed — and then wait in the green room. Panels with multiple remote speakers get a group check together, because the problems (talking over each other, latency, who looks at what) only appear in combination.</p>
<h2>Platform go-live rehearsal</h2>
<p>If you're using an event platform, rehearse the audience's experience, not just the broadcast: log in as a delegate, find the session, watch the stream, submit a question, answer the poll. Corporate networks block things; discover it on Tuesday, not on the morning.</p>
<h2>A rehearsal schedule that works</h2>
<table><tr><th>When</th><th>What</th><th>Who</th></tr>
<tr><td>7–10 days out</td><td>Remote speaker one-to-one checks</td><td>Speaker wrangler, each speaker</td></tr>
<tr><td>2–3 days out</td><td>Platform walkthrough as a delegate</td><td>Producer, client</td></tr>
<tr><td>Day before (or −3h)</td><td>Tech run: every source, every route, test stream</td><td>Full crew</td></tr>
<tr><td>Day before (or −2h)</td><td>Dress rehearsal with failure drills</td><td>Full crew, presenters</td></tr>
<tr><td>−30 min</td><td>Line checks, holding loop live, comms check</td><td>Full crew, remote speakers</td></tr></table>
<div class="callout"><p>A rehearsal that finds nothing wrong was either perfect or too gentle. Usually the second.</p></div>
'''),

dict(slug='guide-risk-redundancy.html', pillar='pre-production',
 title='Redundancy & Risk Planning for Live Streaming: What Fails and What to Do | Virtual Studio Events',
 desc='A broadcast engineer\'s risk register for virtual and hybrid events: the failures that actually happen, how likely they are, and the redundancy that prevents each one.',
 h1='Redundancy: <span class="em">plan for the failure you\'ll actually get.</span>', h1_plain='Redundancy: plan for the failure you\'ll actually get',
 lede='Everything that has gone wrong on our shows in six years, ranked by how often, with the fix for each.',
 related=['guide-internet-connectivity.html','guide-encoders-bitrates.html','guide-when-it-goes-wrong.html','guide-rehearsals.html'],
 body='''
<p>Redundancy is insurance, and like insurance it's cheap compared with the claim. A second encoder and a bonded backup connection add a few hundred pounds to a production; the alternative is your all-hands going black in front of 3,000 employees. This is our working risk register — the things that actually break, in roughly the order they break.</p>
<h2>1. Internet (the most likely failure by a distance)</h2>
<p><strong>What happens:</strong> venue line saturates, guest Wi-Fi throttles, an IT policy blocks the streaming port, or a contractor unplugs the wrong cable. <strong>The fix:</strong> a dedicated wired circuit for the stream, separate from everything else; a bonded cellular unit as the second path, tested on the day; and encoders that fail over automatically. <a href="guide-internet-connectivity.html">Connectivity in depth</a>.</p>
<h2>2. Remote speaker links</h2>
<p><strong>What happens:</strong> the keynote's home broadband drops, their laptop updates, their kid starts a download. <strong>The fix:</strong> a tested backup route (phone dial-in at minimum), their slides run from the gallery so the story can continue, and a holding graphic with their name ready. For critical speakers: pre-record a version as insurance.</p>
<h2>3. Encoder or production machine</h2>
<p><strong>What happens:</strong> software crashes, a machine overheats, a driver decides today's the day. <strong>The fix:</strong> two encoders running in parallel to the same destination or to a switchable backup ingest; a second production machine mirroring the primary on bigger shows; a hard rule that nothing on the show machines is updated in the 48 hours before the event.</p>
<h2>4. Power</h2>
<p><strong>What happens:</strong> a circuit trips when catering plugs in the urn. <strong>The fix:</strong> UPS units on every critical machine (encoder, switcher, network switch, router) sized for at least 15 minutes; gallery on a separate circuit from front-of-house where the venue allows it.</p>
<h2>5. Audio</h2>
<p><strong>What happens:</strong> a radio mic battery dies mid-keynote; the stream gets room PA feed and sounds like a swimming pool; a remote speaker's return causes echo. <strong>The fix:</strong> fresh batteries at every break, a backup handheld mic on stage, a dedicated stream mix with mix-minus returns, and a sound engineer whose only job is listening to the stream. <a href="guide-stream-audio.html">Stream audio explained</a>.</p>
<h2>6. Platform and CDN</h2>
<p><strong>What happens:</strong> the event platform has an outage, or a corporate network blocks the player. <strong>The fix:</strong> stream to a second destination (an unlisted YouTube or Vimeo link) that can be emailed to attendees in minutes; test the platform on the client's own corporate network in advance.</p>
<h2>7. Playback and content</h2>
<p><strong>What happens:</strong> a VT arrives in the wrong format, a slide deck has fonts missing, the file "was definitely in the folder". <strong>The fix:</strong> content deadline 48 hours before, everything transcoded to a house format and checked, no content accepted on USB on the morning without a test play.</p>
<h2>8. People</h2>
<p><strong>What happens:</strong> a crew member is ill; the presenter is stuck on a train. <strong>The fix:</strong> crew depth on shows that matter (a second operator who could take over), a standby presenter briefed, and a run order that anyone can pick up because it's written properly.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Budget rule:</strong> redundancy on the internet path and the encoder is never optional on a show with an audience over 100. Everything else scales with what the event is worth to you.</p></div>
'''),

dict(slug='guide-accessibility.html', pillar='pre-production',
 title='Accessible Virtual Events: Captions, BSL, Audio Description & WCAG | Virtual Studio Events',
 desc='How to make a virtual or hybrid event accessible: live captions vs auto-captions, BSL interpretation on stream, audio description, accessible platforms and slides, and UK legal context.',
 h1='Accessibility: <span class="em">baseline, not bonus.</span>', h1_plain='Accessibility: baseline, not bonus',
 lede='Captions, BSL, audio description and accessible platforms — what good looks like, what it costs and how to build it into the production.',
 related=['guide-engagement-features.html','guide-virtual-event-platform.html','guide-graphics-lower-thirds.html'],
 body='''
<p>In 2026 accessibility isn't an add-on; it's an expectation from audiences, procurement teams and, for public-sector and many corporate clients, a requirement. The good news is that most of it is a production decision made early and delivered routinely — and it improves the event for everyone.</p>
<h2>Captions: live human, live automated, or both</h2>
<p><strong>Automated live captions</strong> (from the platform or an AI service) are now good enough for a clear single speaker in a quiet studio, and free or cheap. They struggle with panels, accents, jargon and product names. <strong>Human live captioners</strong> (stenographers or respeakers) deliver 98%+ accuracy and handle names and terminology from a glossary you supply, at a cost of a few hundred pounds per hour. Our recommendation: human captions for anything public-facing or legally sensitive; automated for internal events with a clear speaker — and always burn corrected captions into the on-demand version afterwards.</p>
<h2>BSL interpretation on the stream</h2>
<p>A British Sign Language interpreter needs to be visible on screen throughout, not just when "important" things happen. In production terms that means a dedicated camera or remote feed for the interpreter, composited as a picture-in-picture that the vision mixer never removes, in a position that doesn't collide with lower thirds. Book interpreters in pairs for anything over an hour — they work in 20-minute rotations. Send them the run order and scripts in advance.</p>
<h2>Audio description and visual content</h2>
<p>Every visual element needs to be understandable by ear. Presenters describe what's on their slides ("this chart shows revenue doubling between 2023 and 2025"). Videos with on-screen text get a voiceover or a described version. Polls are read out. This is a briefing note to presenters, not a technical cost — and it makes the audio-only listener, the person on a bad connection and the person on a treadmill all better served.</p>
<h2>The platform and the player</h2>
<p>Check that the platform is keyboard-navigable, works with screen readers, lets viewers turn captions on and resize them, and has adequate colour contrast. Ask vendors for their accessibility statement or VPAT. If they don't have one, that's an answer.</p>
<h2>Slides and graphics</h2>
<p>Minimum 24pt text on slides that will be streamed; high contrast; no information carried by colour alone; lower thirds on screen long enough to be read twice. A slide that's fine in a boardroom is unreadable on a phone stream.</p>
<h2>Timing, breaks and content warnings</h2>
<p>Breaks every 60–90 minutes; agendas published with times; content warnings for flashing imagery (and avoid it — strobe effects in stings are a real risk on screen). Provide transcripts and slide decks afterwards.</p>
<h2>The legal context (briefly)</h2>
<p>In the UK the Equality Act 2010 requires reasonable adjustments for disabled people, and public-sector bodies must meet the Public Sector Bodies Accessibility Regulations, which reference WCAG 2.1 AA. Whether or not a particular event is strictly caught, procurement increasingly asks the question. This is general information, not legal advice — but building captions and accessible platforms in by default means you never have to work out the answer under pressure.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Ask for captions, BSL and an accessible platform in the brief. If a supplier hesitates, they haven't done it before.</p></div>
'''),

dict(slug='guide-live-streaming-cost.html', pillar='pre-production',
 title='How Much Does Live Streaming an Event Cost? UK Prices 2026 | Virtual Studio Events',
 desc='Honest UK pricing for event live streaming and virtual event production: what drives cost, typical budgets from single-camera webinars to multi-camera hybrid broadcasts, and how to reduce it.',
 h1='What does live streaming <span class="em">actually cost?</span>', h1_plain='What does live streaming an event actually cost?',
 lede='Honest UK numbers and what drives them — so you can budget before you ask anyone for a quote.',
 related=['guide-virtual-event-production.html','guide-risk-redundancy.html','guide-cloud-production.html'],
 body='''
<p>Streaming costs scale with four things: <strong>cameras, hours, destinations and risk</strong>. Here's how that plays out in the UK market in 2026. All figures exclude VAT and assume a single show day; use our <a href="tools.html#budget">budget estimator</a> for a number tailored to your event.</p>
<h2>Simple webinar or boardroom stream — roughly £750–£1,500</h2>
<p>One camera, slides, a streaming engineer with an encoder, one destination (Teams, YouTube, LinkedIn). The cost is mostly the engineer's day and the kit. Add a remote presenter and it edges toward the top of the range.</p>
<h2>Single-room conference or town hall — roughly £2,000–£5,000</h2>
<p>Two to three cameras, vision mixing, a dedicated stream audio mix, graphics and lower thirds, redundant encoding, possibly a remote speaker or two, a crew of two to three. This is the most common brief we see, and the range reflects how much redundancy and graphics polish the client wants.</p>
<h2>Hybrid conference or awards show — £5,000–£15,000+</h2>
<p>Multi-camera, full graphics package, an event platform with registration and Q&amp;A, remote contributors managed on broadcast links, a rehearsal day, redundant everything. The platform and the rehearsal day are the items people forget to budget, and the two that most affect whether the show goes well.</p>
<h2>What moves the number up</h2>
<ul><li>Multiple sessions or rooms streamed simultaneously (each room is effectively its own production).</li><li>Pre-records and edit work before the event.</li><li>Custom platform builds or complex registration.</li><li>International remote speakers on managed links, with out-of-hours rehearsals.</li><li>Same-day highlight edits and social cuts.</li><li>Human live captioning and BSL interpretation.</li></ul>
<h2>What moves it down</h2>
<ul><li>A venue whose in-house AV can supply a clean audio split and camera feeds.</li><li>Flexible timings that let a crew do the tech run the same day.</li><li>Using a <a href="guide-cloud-production.html">cloud gallery</a> instead of physical production kit on site.</li><li>Remote operation — a remote vMix operator runs the show for a fraction of an on-site crew.</li><li>Standard graphics templates rather than bespoke motion packages.</li></ul>
<h2>Day rates you'll see on quotes</h2>
<p>UK broadcast crew rates in 2026 typically sit in these bands: streaming or vision engineers £400–£550 per day; video HOD or technical director £550–£700; production manager £500–£650; editor £350–£500. Production machines (a vMix system, say) run £350–£500 per day; bonded cellular encoders £250–£550 per job. Travel, accommodation and consumables are billed at cost. Multi-day bookings usually attract a reduction.</p>
<h2>The cheapest insurance you can buy</h2>
<p>Redundancy — a second encoder and a bonded backup connection — typically adds a few hundred pounds. Set that against the cost of your event going dark in front of the whole company and it's the easiest line on the budget to approve.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Want a tailored number? The <a href="tools.html#budget">budget estimator</a> takes 60 seconds, or <a href="contact.html">send us the brief</a> for a real quote within a working day.</p></div>
'''),
]
